================================================================================
OTHER UPSTREAM SOURCES
================================================================================

  SLATEC (public domain, no derivative obligation) is a permitted source where
  GSL lacks a routine — e.g. CSEVL/DCSEVL and INITS/INITDS.

  GNU Octave (GPL-3.0-or-later) is a secondary source, but its special
  functions are largely SLATEC-derived (liboctave/external/slatec-fn/); for
  those, port from SLATEC directly rather than inheriting Octave's chain. Only
  genuinely Octave-original routines should be taken from Octave. Neither is
  vendored at present.

================================================================================
IN-WORKSPACE UPSTREAMS (lifted verbatim)
================================================================================

  Beyond the GSL ports above, PETIR carries code lifted BYTE-FOR-BYTE from two
  other crates in this workspace. Both are GPL-3.0-only and authored here, so
  no new licence obligation arises -- but the provenance still travels with
  every file, and `tests/verbatim_provenance.rs` checks mechanically that the
  copies have not drifted from their sources.

  outram-foam-basic-lib (GPL-3.0-only; derived from OpenFOAM,
  Copyright (C) 2004-2023 OpenFOAM Foundation and 2016-2023 OpenCFD Ltd)

    src/matrix/square_matrix.rs        -> linalg/square_matrix.rs
    src/polynomial/{roots, linear_eqn,
      quadratic_eqn, cubic_eqn,
      polynomial}.rs                   -> poly/
    src/math/erf_inv.rs                -> specfunc/erf_inv.rs
    src/math/inc_gamma.rs              -> specfunc/inc_gamma.rs
    src/math/inv_inc_gamma.rs          -> specfunc/inv_inc_gamma.rs
    src/primitives/scalar.rs           -> scalar.rs

  The two incomplete-gamma files carry the ONE substantive deviation in the
  whole set: upstream declares erf/erfc/tgamma/lgamma as `extern "C"` and calls
  the platform libm through `unsafe`. PETIR cannot -- a no_std target has no
  libm to link and wasm32-unknown-unknown has no libm symbols at all -- so those
  calls route through the pure-Rust `libm` crate instead. Same fdlibm/musl
  lineage, and the `unsafe` disappears.

  chem-eng-real-time-process-control-simulator (GPL-3.0-only; itself a port of
  the GNU Octave control package, GPL-3.0-or-later)

    src/lib/beta_testing/z_domain/*.rs -> transfer_fn/
    src/lib/beta_testing/
      stable_transfer_functions/*.rs   -> transfer_fn/

  Not lifted: that crate's `transfer_fn_wrapper_and_enums` layer, which writes
  simulation output through `csv::Writer` -- a std-and-filesystem dependency
  with no place in a no_std numerics crate.

================================================================================
DEPENDENCY LICENCES
================================================================================

  libm  MIT OR Apache-2.0 — a pure-Rust port of musl's libm. Permissive into
        GPL-3.0. Required, not optional: in no_std the f64 transcendentals are
        std-only, and GSL calls them throughout.

  uom   Apache-2.0 OR MIT — dimensional analysis. Permissive into GPL-3.0.
        OPTIONAL, behind the default-on `transfer-fn` feature, and used only by
        the transfer-function blocks, where a sample time genuinely is a Time
        and a signal genuinely is a Ratio. Every numerics layer takes bare f64.
        Built with default-features off: uom's defaults include `std`.

